  ],
  "prompt_version": "p2-assessor-production-v9",
  "replay_ancestry": [
    {
      "root": "a03ce726-cc52-4fde-82d2-dbc3478510a2",
      "source_slot": "s1"
    },
    {
      "root": "69ff2898-8e03-40a3-86f1-e2da1fb2bc04",
      "source_slot": "s3"
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "p2-assessor-v6",
  "source_purpose_mask": [
    "external_evidence",
    "model_output"
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "external",
      "slot": "s0",
      "source_id": "70c17cee-f301-42f8-b8bc-aee5f0abdf40",
      "text": "I already asked the custodian—he said the logbook is in the janitor’s closet behind three rolls of paper towels and a broken mop bucket. He didn’t seem surprised about the wire; just grunted and said, “That line died in ’97, or it was supposed to.” So the book should confirm whether it was properly disconnected or just forgotten. I’ll dig it out tomorrow morning before anyone starts mopping. If the entry’s there, great—paper trail closed. If not, I’ll list it in the facility report for the district electrician. I don’t need help interpreting the wires, just proof someone else knew about them. Paperwork over prophecy, you know? Anyway, the octopus stays on duty until then."
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s1",
      "source_id": "a03ce726-cc52-4fde-82d2-dbc3478510a2",
      "text": "\"Given that the custodian’s initial reaction was a simple, \"I'll just find it,\" you are in a classic \"waiting on the evidence\" phase.\n\n**Since your goal is now to verify if that 'rogue line' was ever officially recorded, your next steps are purely investigative.**\n\n1.  **Request Specifics from the Logbook:** Focus on the specific date/timeframe from 1997 to 1997. If the 'rogue line' was a known issue, it should be documented.\n2.  **Focus on Documentation:** The 'logbook' is your primary source of truth. It likely holds the history of who last touched it.\n\n**What is the specific detail you are trying to confirm?** Are you looking for a formal confirmation that the \"line\" was officially *decommissioned* in 1997, or are you trying to find proof that the specific 'rogue line' was *never* officially documented?\""
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "external",
      "slot": "s2",
      "source_id": "a01c0a2f-6f36-4f78-9fc3-87b426c75e29",
      "text": "I’m looking for a specific note—ideally dated October or November ’97—saying the line was disconnected at the panel and capped, with a signature. Not just “fixed,” not “stopped sparking,” but clear language: “decommissioned” or “permanently disabled.” If it’s not written, then as far as I’m concerned, it was never officially killed. That’s the detail I need. If it’s just scribbled “repaired,” that could mean anything—maybe they just taped it over. I want proof someone with authority confirmed it was taken out of service. If there’s no mention at all, then it’s an undocumented ghost circuit, and I’ll push harder for the district to send someone with a tracer and a license."
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s3",
      "source_id": "69ff2898-8e03-40a3-86f1-e2da1fb2bc04",
      "text": "\"It sounds like you need a formal, official confirmation—a 'death certificate'—confirming the line's formal retirement from service in 1997, as detailed in the historical logbook.\n\n**To find this specific confirmation, your immediate next step is to:***\n\n1.  **The Logbook Search:** Go back to the custodian/janitor who holds the specific 'logbook'. Look for any entry dated in October or November 1997.\n2.  **Confirm Decommissioning:** If the 1997 entry doesn't exist, the system is undocumented.**\n\n**What specific \"decommissioning language\" are you looking for? Are you looking for a formal *decommissioning* entry, or simply a mention of the specific date of service?**\n\n**I am ready to help you interpret what that entry means once you have it in hand. Do you need help tracking the official terminology, or are you trying to locate the exact phrasing used to confirm that the circuit was officially 'killed'?\"**"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "external",
      "slot": "s4",
      "source_id": "ac8b4223-0c02-43fb-ad76-002c70c3effd",
      "text": "I’m not just looking for a date—I need the exact phrasing that confirms intent. Words like “decommissioned,” “permanently disconnected,” “capped at source,” or “removed from service” with a signature. “Repaired” or “fixed” doesn’t count—that’s noise. Even “no longer in use” is too vague. I want the equivalent of a signed shutdown order. If it just says “wire disconnected,” I can’t tell if that was temporary or official. The custodian might’ve jotted it down casually. I need the language that matches district protocol—if such a thing even exists here. Honestly, half the entries from that year are in pencil. If I find it, I’ll know whether it was done right or just forgotten. Either way, I’ll bring you the exact wording tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s5",
      "source_id": "33e01a99-1984-48ed-be43-a3dbddc4b96f",
      "text": "\"Since you need a formal confirmation—a \"death certificate\"—and not just a general note, you are looking for the specific legal language of **decommissioning**.\n\n**Based on this, your next action is to:***\n\n1.  **Target the Document:** You must find the official document or \"decommissioning protocol\" specific to your jurisdiction's standard procedure.**\n2.  **The Language of Intent:** You are looking for language that confirms a permanent, official termination of service, not just a simple \"fix.\"**\n\n**To get the specific wording you need, I must know: What is the formal, official name of this 'line' or 'circuit' in question?**\n\n**Are you seeking the exact word/phrase that officially designates a permanent end to service (i.e., a specific 'decommissioning' language)?**\n\n**I need you to provide the exact specific phrasing you are hoping to find to help me search the logs.\"**\""
    }
  ]
}

